//Object
An object is an instance of a class.
The word “instance” generally means an example or occurrence of something.

In OOP:

Instance = a particular object created from a class.

For example, s1 is an instance of the Student class.

So whenever you hear “instance of a class”, just think:

“An actual object made from that class.”
If a class is the blueprint, an object is the actual thing created using that blueprint.

Example:

class Student {
public:
    string name;
    int age;
};

Here Student is the class.

Now:

Student s1;
Student s2;

s1 and s2 are objects of the Student class.

Each object has its own copy of the non-static data members:

s1.name = "Shubham";
s1.age = 20;

s2.name = "Rahul";
s2.age = 21;

So:

Student → Class / Blueprint

s1 → Object
     name = Shubham
     age = 20

s2 → Object
     name = Rahul
     age = 21

Objects can access the class's public members using the dot (.) operator:

s1.name = "Shubham";
s1.age = 20;
Mental model

Class = blueprint
Object = actual instance created from that blueprint

That's the core of Object.

Class
class Student {
public:
    string name;
    int age;
};

This is the blueprint.

It says:

Every Student object will have a name and an age.

Now create 2 objects
Student s1;
Student s2;

You have made two separate objects.

Think of it like making two boxes from the same blueprint:

        Student class
        (blueprint)
          /     \
         /       \
        ↓         ↓
      s1 box    s2 box

Each box has its own name and age:
Objects can access public members using .
s1                    s2
┌──────────┐          ┌──────────┐
│ name     │          │ name     │
│ age      │          │ age      │
└──────────┘          └──────────┘

Now:

s1.age = 20;
s2.age = 25;

You get:

s1                    s2
┌──────────┐          ┌──────────┐
│ age = 20 │          │ age = 25 │
└──────────┘          └──────────┘

They are completely separate.

If you do:

s1.age = 30;

only s1 changes:

s1                    s2
┌──────────┐          ┌──────────┐
│ age = 30 │          │ age = 25 │
└──────────┘          └──────────┘
So what does "copy" mean?

When we say each object has its own copy of the data member, we mean:

s1.age and s2.age are two separate variables.

It's not:

s1.age ───────→ same age ←─────── s2.age

Instead:

s1.age = 20

s2.age = 25

Two separate pieces of data.

One correction to your original wording

Don't say:

"Object carries a copy of the class."

Say:

"Each object has its own copy of the class's non-static data members."

That's the important idea.

Don't worry about references yet. We'll encounter references later when we study pointers/references.

1. Non-static data member

This is the normal variable inside a class.

class Student {
public:
    string name;
    int age;
};

Here:

name → non-static data member
age  → non-static data member

Every object gets its own separate copy.

Student s1;
Student s2;

s1.age = 20;
s2.age = 25;

Think:

s1                  s2
┌─────────┐         ┌─────────┐
│ age = 20 │         │ age = 25 │
└─────────┘         └─────────┘
   ↑                    ↑
 separate              separate
2. Static data member

Yes, we can also make a variable static:

class Student {
public:
    static int count;
};

Now count is shared by all objects.

Instead of:

s1 → its own count
s2 → its own count
s3 → its own count

there is one common count:

             count
               ↑
        ┌──────┼──────┐
        ↓      ↓      ↓
       s1     s2     s3

For example, if:

Student::count = 3;

then all objects see the same count.

The easiest difference
NON-STATIC                    STATIC

s1 → own age                  s1 ─┐
s2 → own age                  s2 ─┼→ same count
s3 → own age                  s3 ─┘
Example
class Student {
public:
    int age;             // non-static
    static int count;    // static
};

If you create:

Student s1;
Student s2;

then:

s1 → age      ← separate
s2 → age      ← separate

s1 ─┐
s2 ─┼→ count  ← shared
For now, remember:

Non-static = each object has its own copy.

Static = one shared copy for the entire class.

We'll study static members properly later as its own topic. For the Object topic, just knowing this distinction is enough.